    "text": "A silver ball of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>4.8</mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi>cm</mi></math> is suspended by a thread in a vacuum chamber. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>UV</mi></math>&nbsp;light of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>200</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi>nm</mi></math> is incident on the ball for some time during which total energy of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>–</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math> falls on the surface. Assuming that on an average, one out of <math><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math> photons incident on the ball is able to eject an electron, then the potential of the sphere will be",
